# (perf-measure-parity) A CHECKOUT HOLDS THE REPOSITORY'S BYTES, ON EVERY MACHINE.
# MEASURED 2026-10-02 (9e0662a9): the Windows development machine has core.autocrlf=true, which put a
# carriage return before every line break of every text file this file did not name — and the build
# copies data/ and the static pages into dist/ VERBATIM, so the same commit built locally weighed
# 121,742 bytes more in dist/ (85,104 of them in data/) than the identical build on CI, whose checkout
# is the blob bytes. Nothing is served from a Windows checkout: the deploy is CI's build, so the
# carriage returns were bytes no reader is ever handed, and every size measured here was a size of
# nothing. `text=auto` is the same detection core.autocrlf=true already applies (a file with a NUL or a
# lone CR is binary and never converted, and a file committed WITH CRLF is left as committed — git's
# own rule for text=auto); `eol=lf` makes the checkout write the blob back unchanged. Attributes
# override core.autocrlf, so this needs no per-machine configuration. A tree checked out before this
# line keeps its CRLF until git rewrites each file (git status reads it as clean); the bundler's half
# of the build never depended on it (measured: eager and async identical from both checkouts).
*      text=auto eol=lf

# Force Unix (LF) line endings for files that are executed or parsed on Linux
# (CI runners, Supabase, servers). A CRLF here can break bash / psql / YAML.
# (Forced `text`, not `auto`: these are LF even if a CRLF was committed.)
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.sql  text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf

# ── (generated-file-merge-driver) HOW A MERGE RESOLVES WHAT NO PERSON WRITES ────────────────────
# MEASURED 2026-10-01: fifteen pull requests landing in parallel conflicted almost only in the files
# below, and each one was resolved by hand with the same mechanical procedure. scripts/merge-driver.mjs
# is that procedure; this block is the ONE declaration of it (git requires the path list here, so the
# kind and the regeneration command live on the same line rather than in a second list).
# The driver itself is configuration — `node scripts/merge-driver.mjs --install` (worktree.mjs and
# master-sync.mjs run it). tests/generated-file-merge-driver-checks.test.mjs discovers every file that
# DECLARES ITSELF generated or a ledger (a GENERATED/生成物 header naming its script, a GENERATED … BEGIN
# region, a ledger whose "//" or "_" names its writer) and fails while one is missing here.
#   json    a ledger: three-way by key and by member; a number both sides moved → intmap-clash
#           (sum = add both moves, a count; upstream = take main's value, a measurement). Never regenerated.
#           intmap-clash-by=<script> lets the ledger's WRITER name the rule per row (its mergeClash(keys)),
#           for a ledger that holds both counts and measurements.
#   regen   a function of tracked inputs: hunks inside the generated region take main's side, and
#           `node scripts/merge-driver.mjs --finish` runs intmap-regen after the merge (`!` = print, do not run)
#   tokens  hand-written text that states counts: an integer both sides moved → base + both moves,
#           an insertion both sides made at one place → both (main's first). Anything else is a conflict.
# intmap-regen: commands separated by `;`, words by `,` (an attribute value has no spaces).

